=== L5 Notable Women & the Sources ===
id: 1ef1cc94-e07a-4aee-88df-585f8669535d
slug: notable-women-the-sources
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:24:41.893941+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 21

--- DESCRIPTION ---
Remarkable women of antiquity and the critical problem of using male-authored sources to study women's lives.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">The Problem of the Sources</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">Every ancient text we have on women was written by a man. In Athens and Rome, literacy was predominantly a male achievement; writing history, biography, comedy, and philosophy was an activity performed almost entirely by men of the educated classes. This is not a trivial problem: it means that every portrait of a real woman that survives &mdash; praising Cornelia as the ideal mother, condemning Clodia as immoral, sympathising with Neaira as a victim &mdash; is filtered through a male perspective that had its own values, anxieties, and purposes. Understanding the <dfn class="term" data-def="The tendency of a source to present information from a particular viewpoint shaped by the author's background, purpose, or culture.">bias</dfn> in a source is not the same as dismissing it; a biased source still tells us things, but we must ask what it is actually evidence <em>for</em>.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n3">Cornelia: the Ideal Roman Mother</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n4"><dfn class="term" data-def="The daughter of Scipio Africanus and mother of the Gracchi brothers (Tiberius and Gaius Gracchus), celebrated in Roman tradition as the ideal of educated, virtuous Roman motherhood.">Cornelia</dfn>, daughter of the great general Scipio Africanus and mother of the Gracchi brothers (the reforming tribunes Tiberius and Gaius Gracchus), was one of the most celebrated women in Roman tradition. After her husband&rsquo;s death, she refused a second marriage &mdash; even, allegedly, a proposal from the Egyptian king Ptolemy VIII &mdash; and devoted herself to her children&rsquo;s education. A famous story, told by the ancient author Valerius Maximus and repeated by others, describes a visitor showing off her jewellery, and Cornelia gesturing to her children: &ldquo;These are my jewels.&rdquo; Whether this happened or not, the anecdote perfectly encapsulates what Roman culture wanted a great woman to be: a mother whose only ornament was her sons&rsquo; excellence.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n5">Cornelia was also known in antiquity for her education. The Roman author and lawyer Cicero praised the quality of her Latin style in a collection of her letters, though those letters no longer survive. Her reputation for learning is significant: in the Roman world, a woman&rsquo;s education was generally directed towards making her a better wife and mother. But Cornelia&rsquo;s intellectual cultivation was taken seriously in its own right, and she was used in Roman culture as proof that high female education was not threatening if it was properly channelled through the maternal role.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n6" data-revision-tip="Cover this and recall: who was Cornelia, who were her famous sons, and what anecdote made her a symbol of Roman ideal motherhood?">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Cornelia was the daughter of Scipio Africanus and mother of the reforming tribunes Tiberius and Gaius Gracchus. The famous story of her pointing to her children as &ldquo;my jewels&rdquo; made her the Roman ideal of the devoted, educated mother. She refused a second marriage to focus on her sons&rsquo; upbringing.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n7">Lucretia: Virtue, Rape and the Foundation of the Republic</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n8"><dfn class="term" data-def="A noblewoman of early Rome whose rape by Tarquinius Superbus's son and subsequent suicide led to the expulsion of the kings and the founding of the Republic, according to the tradition recorded by Livy.">Lucretia</dfn> is one of the most celebrated figures of Roman legend. According to the tradition recorded by Livy (<em>History of Rome</em> 1.57&ndash;59, a prescribed source), Lucretia was the wife of a Roman nobleman and the model of wifely virtue. When the Roman princes were boasting about their wives&rsquo; qualities, Lucretia was found spinning wool with her maids at night while other wives were at dinner parties. Her modesty and industry were the proof of her virtue. Her rape by Sextus Tarquinius, the son of Rome&rsquo;s last king, outraged the Roman nobility. Lucretia called her father and husband, told them what had happened, and stabbed herself, declaring that she would not provide a precedent for unchaste Roman women. Her death triggered the revolt led by Brutus that expelled the Tarquin dynasty and established the Roman Republic.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n9">Livy&rsquo;s account is a prescribed literary source and should be read carefully. It is written six centuries after the events he describes, and the story of Lucretia serves a clear political purpose in his narrative: to explain the founding of the Republic as a moral as well as political revolution. Lucretia&rsquo;s importance in Roman culture lay not in her political agency (she died) but in her role as a symbol of what Roman virtue was and why Rome&rsquo;s monarchy was intolerable. Her suicide is presented not as defeat but as an act of supreme moral courage that forced Rome&rsquo;s men to act. Modern readers often note, however, that the story ultimately places the burden of maintaining sexual virtue on the woman, not the man who raped her.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Neaira and Aspasia: two Athenian women outside the respectable circle</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n10"><dfn class="term" data-def="A woman in ancient Athens who lived outside the household and had paid or semi-permanent relationships with men, occupying a higher social position than a simple prostitute but without citizen rights.">Hetairai</dfn> (singular: <em>hetaira</em>) were women who maintained long-term relationships with men outside of marriage &mdash; educated companions who attended symposia (drinking parties), were expected to be witty and musically accomplished, and who occupied an ambiguous social position. They were not citizens; they had no kyrios (male guardian) in the conventional sense; they were both admired and condemned. The most famous was Aspasia of Miletus, companion of Pericles, whom we encountered in the previous lesson. Another figure preserved in the sources is Neaira, whose entire history we know only because she was the subject of a court speech (<em>Against Neaira</em>), attributed to Demosthenes, in which her former partner sought to humiliate her former companion by proving she was a former slave and prostitute who had fraudulently posed as an Athenian citizen. Even this hostile source, designed to ruin her reputation, inadvertently preserves details of Neaira&rsquo;s life that make her one of the most fully documented ordinary women of the ancient world.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n11">Literary versus Material Sources: What Can Each Tell Us?</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n12">Literary sources can be detailed, narrative, and contextual &mdash; but they are biased, written by men, and often designed to make a moral or political argument rather than to describe reality. Material sources (sculpture, painted pottery, inscribed stelai, frescoes) were made by men too, but they were produced for different purposes &mdash; commemoration, display, religious offering &mdash; and they can corroborate or challenge the literary record in valuable ways.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n13">The <dfn class="term" data-def="A marble grave relief from Athens (c. 410 BC), now in the National Archaeological Museum, Athens, commemorating a woman named Hegeso; it shows her seated, choosing jewellery from a box held by a standing slave girl. The sculptor is unknown.">Hegeso Stele</dfn> (now in the National Archaeological Museum, Athens) is a prescribed visual source. Dating to around 410 BC, it shows a seated woman identified by the inscription as Hegeso, daughter of Proxenos, elegantly dressed and examining a piece of jewellery from a decorated box that a standing slave girl holds out to her. The carving is sensitive and restrained: Hegeso is shown as dignified, leisured, and wealthy. The choice of jewellery as her defining activity connects to the ideals of wifely beauty and adornment, but it also shows a woman with resources and domestic authority. Comparing Hegeso&rsquo;s stele with the literary portrait of the respectable Athenian wife &mdash; confined to the home, managing the household &mdash; gives the ideal a face and a presence.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n14">The <dfn class="term" data-def="A marble grave relief from Athens, now in the Kerameikos Museum, showing a woman holding a baby with a small child beside her.">Ampharete Stele</dfn> (Kerameikos Museum, Athens) is another prescribed material source. It depicts a woman holding a small baby on her lap, with an inscription that records the speaker&rsquo;s love for the child: &ldquo;I hold here the dear child of my daughter, whom I held on my knees while alive and looking on the sunlight; now dead, I hold here the dead.&rdquo; This inscription (unlike most funerary stelai, which simply name the deceased) gives us a direct expression of grandmother&rsquo;s grief and love. The material source, combined with its inscription, provides a glimpse of emotional experience that the literary sources rarely offer for real, non-famous women.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n15" data-revision-tip="Cover this and recall: what does the Hegeso Stele show, where is it held, and what does it tell us that literary sources alone cannot?">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The Hegeso Stele (c. 410 BC, National Archaeological Museum, Athens) depicts a seated Athenian woman choosing jewellery from a box held by a slave girl. As a prescribed material source it gives the ideal of the respectable Athenian wife a visual presence, showing her wealth, dignity and domestic authority in a way that literary sources only describe abstractly.</p>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>The &lsquo;Sappho&rsquo; fresco and educated women in Pompeii</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n16">A fresco from Pompeii (now in the National Archaeological Museum of Naples) depicts a young woman holding a set of writing tablets to her lips and a stylus, gazing thoughtfully as if in the process of composing something. The fresco is often called the &ldquo;Sappho&rdquo; fresco because of its association with female literary creativity, though the woman depicted is not identified as the Greek poet. As a prescribed visual source it is significant because it shows a woman of clearly high status (fine jewellery, elaborate hair) in the act of literary or intellectual work: she is not weaving, or managing a slave, or adorning herself, but <em>thinking and writing</em>. This challenges a simple picture of Roman women as entirely confined to domestic activities and provides evidence for educated, literate women at Pompeii. The visual source complements the literary evidence of Cornelia&rsquo;s praised writing style, suggesting that female literacy, while not universal, was valued in the upper classes of both Republican and Imperial Rome.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n18">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n19">Almost all our literary sources on women were written by men with specific cultural purposes: Livy&rsquo;s Lucretia is a moral archetype for the founding of the Republic; Cornelia&rsquo;s &ldquo;jewels&rdquo; anecdote encapsulates the ideal of the educated Roman mother; both portraits tell us what Roman men valued, not necessarily how these women experienced their own lives.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n20">Material sources &mdash; the Hegeso Stele, the Ampharete Stele, the Pompeii &lsquo;Sappho&rsquo; fresco &mdash; give real women a visual presence, show them with dignity and individuality, and can challenge or corroborate the literary record. The Ampharete inscription preserves a grandmother&rsquo;s grief in her own words, which is rare and precious.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n21">The honest approach to studying women in the ancient world is to acknowledge the limitations of the sources, read each for its purpose and bias, and use literary and material evidence together to build the most complete picture possible.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n17">This topic is particularly well suited to questions about how we use evidence. When a question asks you to &lsquo;Analyse&rsquo; a source such as the Hegeso Stele or the Ampharete Stele, go beyond simply describing what is shown: ask what the patron who commissioned it <em>wanted</em> the viewer to see, and compare it with what literary sources say about women in the same period. For essays asking how far we can trust the evidence for women&rsquo;s lives, the key structure is: literary sources are biased but informative; material sources are less obviously partisan but were also made by men for specific purposes; together they give a more nuanced picture than either alone. Always use specific named sources &mdash; Livy on Lucretia, the Hegeso Stele, the Ampharete inscription, the &lsquo;Sappho&rsquo; fresco &mdash; and say both what each source tells you and what it cannot tell you.</p>

--- PRACTICE_QUESTIONS ---
