  "type": "5 marks — Source Analysis",
  "marks": "Mastering: A thorough analysis using multiple details from the passage (the late-night wool-working as the symbol of diligence and domesticity; the contrast with the other wives at dinner parties; the unannounced visit as a test of virtue; the competitive boasting of husbands as revealing Roman attitudes to wives as reflections of male honour). Connects to the broader cultural ideal of the matrona and explains what Livy’s narrative purpose is (the story establishes Lucretia’s virtue as the precondition for the outrage of her rape, which triggers the founding of the Republic). Notes that Livy was writing centuries after the events and that the anecdote is almost certainly legendary. Reaches a supported judgement about what the passage reveals about Roman values.\nSecure: Uses at least two details from the passage and explains what they suggest about Roman ideals of female virtue. Makes a clear point about the contrast between Lucretia and the other wives and connects this to the matrona ideal.\nDeveloping: Identifies one or two details and makes a basic observation, but the analysis of what the passage reveals is limited.\nEmerging: Paraphrases the passage or makes only a general observation about virtue."
